 Please note that `disk_size_limit_bytes` defines the maximum size in bytes for the on-disk cache, while `memory_max_entries` specifies the maximum number of entries for the in-memory cache.
 
+## Time-to-Live (TTL) for Cache Entries
+
+DSPy supports setting a Time-to-Live (TTL) for cache entries. A TTL specifies the maximum duration (in seconds) that a cached entry remains valid. Once the TTL expires, the entry is considered stale and will be evicted from the cache.
+
+### Setting TTL on Cache Configuration
+
+You can set a TTL when configuring the cache:
+
+```python
+import dspy
+
+# Set a TTL of 3600 seconds (1 hour) for all new cache entries
+dspy.configure_cache(ttl=3600)
+
+# Disable expiration (entries never expire)
+dspy.configure_cache(ttl=None)
+```
+
+### Changing TTL Dynamically
+
+You can change the TTL at runtime using `dspy.clients.set_ttl()`:
+
+```python
+import dspy.clients
+
+# Set TTL to 60 seconds
+dspy.clients.set_ttl(60)
+
+# Change to no expiration
+dspy.clients.set_ttl(None)
+```
+
+**Important notes about TTL:**
+- The TTL applies only to new cache entries written after the TTL is set. Existing entries retain their original expiration times.
+- Reads do not restart the TTL timer. An entry expires based solely on when it was written.
+- Overwriting an existing entry starts a new lifetime with the current TTL.
+- Invalid TTL values (zero, negative, non-finite, boolean, or non-numeric) are rejected with a `ValueError`.
+
+### TTL Behavior
+
+- **Memory-only cache**: Entries expire based on the TTL, regardless of whether they were read from disk.
+- **Disk-only cache**: Entries expire based on the TTL stored with them.
+- **Combined cache**: Both memory and disk entries respect the TTL.
+- **Disabled cache**: TTL has no effect when caching is disabled.
+
 ## Understanding and Customizing the Cache
 
 In specific situations, you might want to implement a custom cache, for example, to gain finer control over how cache keys are generated. By default, the cache key is derived from a hash of all request arguments sent to `litellm`, excluding credentials like `api_key`.
